Z||000013||stsmithgt||12-04-2000||11:34 AM||||I had mine fixed a couple years ago.  They nickel and dimed me into five visits and 1400.00 bucks later, which by that time kept me from converting it because it's almost all completely new now.  It's damn cold though.  Was it worth it?  Well have you ever sweated on real sheep skin seat covers.  They start smelling like a wet dog and the smell sticks to your clothing. YUK!  In the winter, they get wet from the rain = stink.  In the summer moisture from sweat = stink.  I would have never bought the real thing if I knew this.  I took them off, but put them back on because my drivers seat needs recovering.  I should have that done soon.  It's worth having a good air conditioner in the Sacramento Valley that's for sure.||||reg||1
Z||000014||DavidHenshaw||12-04-2000||02:06 PM||||Bought my 86 GT in Sept. and ac wasn't working. Saw the ac belt was missing and thought "Oh boy! I'll just put on a belt."<BR>While putting on the belt I notice someone had used bolt cutters to sever the ac lines where they attach to the compressor. Why?<P>Reminds me of the time several years ago when I rented a house I owned to a new renter. Instead of letting me remove a very nice FOR RENT sign I had up in front of the house the idiot renter used pliers to rip the metal for rent sign to shreds. Go figure.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Ed||12-04-2000||02:35 PM||||Only two working A/C's?!!<P>Well, I live in the Sacramento Valley of CA and as Mr. Smith says, A/C is a good idea here.<BR>  <BR>The bad news is, I've replaced two compressors in ten years in my '87 GT.  When the second one went out last year I had the new refrigerant put in.  Works well.<P>But, every car I've owned has had an A/C compressor failure.  Toyota, Dodge (two caravans), I can't remember if my '84 SE's failed, but it probably did!  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||jmw||12-04-2000||03:06 PM||||Add me to the list. It's MURDER here in the summertime....||||reg||1
Z||000017||286SE'S||12-04-2000||03:53 PM||||This thread is making Fieros look bad.<BR>so:<BR>Both of my air conditioners work just fine.<BR>Wonderfull cold air pouring out of all the vents. AHH.. so nice...<BR>It is not worth the misery of having a car without it. Even my old Fiat X1/9 had air.<BR>This is southern California and the last time I had it on was... uh.. last Saturday! Looks like a bad AC is the reason a lot of Fieros sell so cheap. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||ChadMan||12-04-2000||04:05 PM||||Had an 84 Coupe.  A/C worked fine for two years, then gradually got warmer and warmer.  Had it recharged, but the charge only lasted 3 months.<P>My 86 SE had A/C, but it didn't work.  I noticed the belt was missing, so I replaced it.  Had nice cold A/C for two weeks.  Then the compressor seized and broke the belt.  Sold the car before fixing.<P>My 88 Coupe never had A/C.<P>I have a feeling that if it weren't for the rampant gouging of A/C shops and the enviroNazis making R-12 so hard to get, we'd be more likely to fix our A/C.||||reg||1
Z||000019||82-T/A||12-04-2000||05:07 PM||||Well, it kinda sucks.. but you gotta realize two things.<P><BR>1) These are old cars. <BR>2) These cars are very fun to drive, therefore they take more stress than say.. an 89 Corsica or something.<P>So it's only reasonable that like 90% of our A/Cs don't work.<P>Co-incidentally.. the A/C works in both my cars..<P>barely...<P>I paid like $500 something dollars to have it converted over to R34 in my 87 SE / V6, but now the motor is trashed.. so whats the point?<P>The A/C in my 1984 Fiero 2m4 works.. but I think it has a leak because it's not working so great anymore.. and that one is all original.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000020||Fieroboy1||12-04-2000||06:48 PM||||Hey Sqoach....have you checked your coolant level?  If you have no heat unless you rev the motor and the car is overheating regularly you probably are WAAAAY low on coolant.  The heater core derives its heat from the engine coolant if there is a very low level in the engine you won't be getting coolant into the heater core.  Even if the overflow tank is showing coolant open the radiator cap when the car is cold and make sure there is coolant in there.||||reg||1
Z||000021||jlhuber||12-04-2000||07:51 PM||||A/C!?! ...Thats what the t-tops are for.  The A/C on my , uh...ex-'86 GT did not work.  It was said that it only needed to be recharged.  Ah well, such is life.||||reg||1
